\DOC LOCAL_DOUBLE_TWICE4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_DOUBLE_TWICE4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a machine-state {eventually} goal past one inlined "double modulo
p_25519" code block, replacing the current symbolic state by its successor and
adding an assumption characterising the doubled result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} giving the offset/index of the code block to
step over; the second is a {string list} naming the output variable and the
input variable of the doubling step — the first name fixes the variable used
for the new result value in the added assumptions, the second identifies the
source operand appearing in the arithmetic conclusion. A single subgoal
results. The conclusion {eventually x86 (\s'. ...) sk} becomes the same
postcondition applied to the next state {s(k+1)}, the postcondition body being
unchanged. All assumptions about the old state (program counter, register and
memory reads, {bytes_loaded}, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE ... s0 sk} frame)
are discharged and re-asserted for the new state: the program counter is
advanced, the {MAYCHANGE} frame is extended by the memory region written by the
block, flag-read assumptions about the old state are dropped, and a new
memory-read assumption gives the destination buffer the value of the named
output variable. A numeric assumption is also added relating that output
variable to twice the input value modulo {p_25519}, sometimes guarded by a
bound hypothesis on the input, and sometimes stated as an unconditional
congruence. Mathematical side assumptions not mentioning the state are
retained, although their order in the assumption list may change.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, only this single kind of forward-stepping
transformation occurs, always on goals whose conclusion is an {eventually}
statement about a symbolic machine state.

\ENDDOC
